## 3. AVALOQ DOMAIN PRIMER (priors — verify locally in Phase 0)

### 3.1 Platform anatomy

The Avaloq Core Platform (ACP) runs on Oracle. Business logic lives in **PL/SQL** and **Avaloq Script** (a PL/SQL-derived DSL with macro/preprocessor directives). Bank-specific behavior is built through **parameterization** plus a **custom source layer** authored in the Avaloq Workbench and typically exported as a text file tree — which is what you are indexing.

### 3.2 Source kinds you should expect

Map the local reality to these kinds in Phase 0; the `kind` column uses this vocabulary:

| kind | what it is | typical tells |
|---|---|---|
| `script` | Avaloq Script units | `#` macro/preprocessor directives, source header comments, DDIC id literals |
| `plsql_pkg` / `plsql_pkg_body` | PL/SQL package spec/body | `CREATE OR REPLACE PACKAGE [BODY]` |
| `plsql_proc` / `plsql_func` / `plsql_view` / `plsql_trigger` / `plsql_type` | standalone PL/SQL objects | corresponding `CREATE OR REPLACE …` |
| `ddic` | data dictionary: elements, attributes, code tables, codes | dotted element ids (e.g. `bp.name`), code table definitions |
| `task_def` | task engine: task definitions, static/dynamic actions, workflows, order flows | task/workflow keywords, state transitions, script bindings |
| `doc_def` | doc engine: document definitions, templates, text blocks (DocGen) | doc ids, placeholder syntax, layout refs |
| `interface` | AMI message definitions, inbound/outbound mappings, adapters | message ids, field source↔target mappings |
| `report` | report definitions, EOD/batch jobs | report ids, scheduling references |
| `param` | parameterization / customization data exports | structured key-value or object dumps |
| `sql_script` | migration/installation SQL | DML/DDL batches |
| `other` | anything unclassifiable — must stay rare | — |

### 3.3 Core object model vocabulary

These entity abbreviations appear throughout Avaloq code; use them to seed domain classification, and record the local spelling of each in `conventions.md`: `bp` (business partner), `cont` (container), `mac` (money account), `safe` (safekeeping account), `pos` (position), `evt` (event/transaction), `ord` (order), `asset`/`instr` (financial instruments), `md` (market data), `doc` (documents), `msg` (interface messages), `trx` (transaction type context), `stex` (stock exchange), `bank` (own-bank config).

### 3.4 Layering — required metadata

Every source MUST be assigned a `layer`:

- `core` — Avaloq-delivered, never edited by the bank.
- `custom` — bank layer; typically distinguished by a name prefix (bank short code, `i_`, `x_`, or similar) and/or dedicated directories. **The actual rule is bank-specific: derive it from evidence in Phase 0 and write it down.**
- `patch` — release/patch deliveries, if distinguishable.
- `unknown` — allowed only with an `issue` row explaining why.

Custom sources frequently **override or extend** core sources (same logical name, different layer; or registered hook/exit points). Capturing this override relationship is a first-class goal: it is what the bank's developers most need and can least see.

### 3.5 Reference semantics — the edge vocabulary

Extract these edge types (full enum + resolution rules in Appendix C):

`CALLS`, `INCLUDES` (macro/include), `READS_TABLE`, `WRITES_TABLE`, `USES_DDIC`, `USES_CODE_TABLE`, `BINDS_TASK` (task_def → script), `TRIGGERS_WORKFLOW`, `RENDERS_DOC` (logic → doc_def), `MAPS_MSG` (interface mapping → object/field), `OVERRIDES` (custom → core), `DYNAMIC` (dynamic SQL / dynamic dispatch — **flag it, never guess the target**).

